} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/286"} "Krachtsportgebouw, Amsterdam"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/21719"} "18 from 18 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Single elimination tournament."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "It comes as no surprise that the gold medal victory of " [:a {:href "/athletes/4738"} "Bep van Klaveren"] " in the featherweight division was the most popular of the boxing tournament and perhaps of the entire Games. The tough Dutchman had already beaten the representatives of Spain and Great Britain before he faced " [:a {:href "/athletes/8655"} "Harry Devine"] " of the United States in the semi-final round. Van Klaveren started and finished the fight strongly, and his harder punching and higher work rate saw him safely through. " [:a {:href "/athletes/4986"} "Víctor Peralta"] ", from Argentina, lay in wait for the Dutchman in the final and, although reports vary on who deserved the victory, the decision in favor of Van Klaveren led to outrage in the Argentine support. Dutch police were called to calm the disturbances, which went on for some time."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Van Klaveren’s professional career saw him fight on four continents and in many counties over an amazing 27-year span. Winner of national and European lightweight titles in the early 1930s, he campaigned for many years in the United States, and was in America when World War II broke out. After five years service in the Dutch army, he resumed his career and won the national middleweight title. Retirement at the age of 41 proved temporary, as he resumed fighting at the age of 47 and went on a remarkable run of victories that culminated in an attempt at the European welterweight title."] "\n\n" [:p {} "As the only Dutch boxer to have won Olympic gold, he remains a legendary figure in his native country. A statue of him, in fighting pose, was erected in his hometown of Rotterdam after his death, in 1992. A sign of his enduring popularity is that, in 2007, he was voted the “Greatest Rotterdammer” of all time, beating, amongst others, the philosopher and theologian Erasmus."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/4738"} "Bep van Klaveren"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/4986"} "Víctor Peralta"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ARG"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ARG.png"}] "ARG"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/8655"} "Harry Devine"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/7508"} "Luc Biquet"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BEL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BEL.png"}] "BEL"]] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=5"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/2982"} "Kaarlo Väkevä"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FIN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FIN.png"}] "FIN"]] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=5"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/347